🔴 Description

It was observed that the application communicates with the LDAP server over an insecure protocol (LDAP) without using encryption (LDAPS). The communication occurs over default port 389, which transmits data in plaintext. Sensitive information such as credentials and directory queries are exposed during transmission. This indicates lack of secure communication controls for directory services.

🔴 Impact
Credentials can be intercepted by attackers through network sniffing
Enables man-in-the-middle attacks on authentication traffic
Unauthorized access to directory services and user information
🔧 Mitigation
It is recommended to implement LDAPS (LDAP over SSL/TLS) using port 636 to encrypt all directory communications
Configure the application to enforce certificate validation and disable insecure LDAP connections
Ensure all sensitive authentication traffic is transmitted over secure channels only
